            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives the value below which a given share of the values falls. q 0.5 is the median:\nhalf the values are below it. q 0.9 is the value only a tenth exceed - a robust \"loud\" or \"fast\" level that a few outliers cannot drag up the way they drag the maximum. q 0.1 and 0.9 together give a range that ignores the extremes.\n\nUse it to set thresholds from the data itself, to normalise a feature to its typical range (from the 5% to the 95% quantile), or to summarise a distribution.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.quantile <q> <dim>\nDefaults: q 0.5, the whole tensor. q is a share from 0 to 1 (np.percentile uses 0 to\n100). Giving a dim adds dim and keep_dims boxes.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nq (float box, also an inlet):\nThe share, 0 to 1. A list - 0.1 0.5 0.9 - asks for several at once.\n\ndim, keep_dims (also inlets; only when a dim was given):\nEach slice along dim gets its own quantile(s).\n\ninterpolation:\nAn option, under the node: how a q that falls between two values is answered - linear (the default) blends them, lower and higher pick one, nearest the closer, midpoint their mean.\n\nignore nan:\nAn option, under the node: skip nan values (otherwise any nan makes the answer nan).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe quantile(s).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe values 1 to 9. A quarter of the way up is 3, half way 5, three quarters 7.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.percentile is the numpy version (q in 0 to 100). t.median is q 0.5. t.sort puts every value in order. t.histc counts how many fall in each range.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1250,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives the value below which a given share of the values falls. q 0.5 is the median:\nhalf the values are below it. q 0.9 is the value only a tenth exceed - a robust \"loud\" or \"fast\" level that a few outliers cannot drag up the way they drag the maximum. q 0.1 and 0.9 together give a range that ignores the extremes.\n\nUse it to set thresholds from the data itself, to normalise a feature to its typical range (from the 5% to the 95% quantile), or to summarise a distribution.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.quantile <q> <dim>\nDefaults: q 0.5, the whole tensor. q is a share from 0 to 1 (np.percentile uses 0 to\n100). Giving a dim adds dim and keep_dims boxes.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe values. Every input sends.\n\nq (float box, also an inlet):\nThe share, 0 to 1. A list - 0.1 0.5 0.9 - asks for several at once.\n\ndim, keep_dims (also inlets; only when a dim was given):\nEach slice along dim gets its own quantile(s).\n\ninterpolation:\nAn option, under the node: how a q that falls between two values is answered - linear (the default) blends them, lower and higher pick one, nearest the closer, midpoint their mean.\n\nignore nan:\nAn option, under the node: skip nan values (otherwise any nan makes the answer nan).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe quantile(s).\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe values 1 to 9. A quarter of the way up is 3, half way 5, three quarters 7.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.percentile is the numpy version (q in 0 to 100). t.median is q 0.5. t.sort puts every value in order. t.histc counts how many fall in each range."
        },
